\section{Logistic models}{


By default, \code{covlmc} uses two different computing \emph{engines} for logistic
models:
\itemize{
\item when the time series has only two states, \code{covlmc} uses \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}}
with the binomial family (\code{\link[stats:family]{stats::binomial()}});
\item when the time series has at least three
states, \code{covlmc} uses \code{\link[VGAM:vglm]{VGAM::vglm()}} with the multinomial family
(\code{\link[VGAM:multinomial]{VGAM::multinomial()}}).
}

It is possible to replace \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} and \code{\link[VGAM:vglm]{VGAM::vglm()}} with
\code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} by setting the global option \code{mixvlmc.predictive} to
\code{"multinom"} (the default value is \code{"glm"}). The engines estimate the same
models, but their results are not always identical:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} and \code{\link[VGAM:vglm]{VGAM::vglm()}} use iteratively reweighted least
squares while \code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} uses a quasi-Newton optimisation. When
the data of a context are (almost) separable, for instance when a value of
a covariate determines the next state, the maximum likelihood coefficients
are infinite and the engines stop at different (large) values. The
likelihoods remain very close, but the coefficients can be quite different
and a likelihood ratio test whose p-value is close to \code{alpha} can lead to
different decisions, and thus to different models.
\item \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} and \code{\link[VGAM:vglm]{VGAM::vglm()}} report a warning when they do not
converge in \code{mixvlmc.maxit} iterations (see \link{mixvlmc-package}), while
\code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} does not.
\item the states that never follow a context in the data are ignored by the
model of this context, which gives them a zero probability and has fewer
coefficients. Similarly, levels of factor covariates absent from the data
of a context are ignored: \code{\link[VGAM:vglm]{VGAM::vglm()}} reports no coefficient for them
while \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} and \code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} report a zero coefficient (for
instance in \code{\link[=draw.covlmc]{draw.covlmc()}}).
}

As a consequence of the last point, the number of parameters of a COVLMC,
used by \code{\link[stats:AIC]{stats::AIC()}} and \code{\link[stats:BIC]{stats::BIC()}} (see \code{\link[=loglikelihood.covlmc]{loglikelihood.covlmc()}}),
does not depend on the engine.
